This version (refer to it as 910620 b) fixes the two bugs I found:

	1. There was a bug that was hidden if the data part of the disk
started on an even sector number.

	2. There was a short-cut in the code which was not adequately
guarded.

Also, this version has two new features:

	1. If your BIOS sets bit 1 of the flags word of the BPB, it is
taken to mean that the disk in question has only one FAT.  Thus, with an
appropriate Getbpb hook (or a new BIOS :-) you can safely use disks which
have only one FAT.  MSDOS sometimes formats disks with only one FAT. GEMDOS
has always been able to read such disks, but writing to them has been
disasterous.  On disks with more than two FATs, only the last two will be
used.  In any case, only the last FAT (the one abutting the root directory)
will be read; the other FAT will only be written.

	2. When the state of a disk goes to "maybe changed" (i.e. you have
popped out a floppy disk) and there are no dirty buffers on that disk, all
cached data from that disk is forgotten.  This means that removing a disk
from a machine, modifying it on another machine, and putting it back in the
first machine will not cause stale data to be used.  Also, when there are
no open files on the disk in question, removing a disk and replacing it
with another that has the same serial number no longer causes any stale
data to be used.

	Open files are not forgotten.  You can still get stale data if
there are open files on the disk that is removed.  You can actually destroy
data on the new disk if there are dirty buffers destined for the old disk.
Since the only way to have dirty buffers on a disk is to have open files on
the disk which have been written to and not closed, you are safe if you do
not remove a disk that has open files on it.  Thus:

	Thou Shalt Not Remove Disks When Thou Hast Open Files On Them.

	"Removing a disk" does not include swapping logical disks A and B
when instructed to, or removing a disk from the drive (e.g. to change its
write-protect tab or read its label) and putting the same disk back in the
same drive.

	I have found that MSDOS 3.x exhibits the same unsafe behavior, and
this is much safer than old GEMDOS, so it's definitely a net win.

	MSDOS version 5.x (4.x?) puts a serial number on each disk, but not
in the same place that TOS uses.  Most MSDOS disks appear to TOS to have
the same serial number.  A future BIOS will include the MSDOS 5.x serial
number area in the media-change checking code.  This is transparent to
GEMDOS.

	This feature is slightly imperfect: if the disk you put in appears
to have the same serial number, but is not formatted the same way (i.e.
sizes of the FATs and root directory) there will be trouble.  A new BIOS
and/or GEMDOS will address this concern.

